The Prophet Roy L. Fuchs , known to the wise as roylfuchs@urfargingicehole.org, opened the Book of Words, and read unto the people:
> Flounder is a fish.  Founder is what a ship does on a reef or sand
>bar.  Though some idiots have said flounder for so many years that
>both are now accepted.

There are but two rules to pedantry: the first is that you must be
willing to accept the consequences, in particular being regarded as a
jackass. In this you seem adequately well-equipped. However, the
second, and far more important, rule is that you absolutely must be
correct, else you are not only a jackass, but a hypocritical, foolish
jackass. With regards to this rule, you're not quite up to scratch.

"Founder" and "flounder", for a start, do not have the same
usage. Both can be used nautically, but "founder" is roughly a synonym
of "capsize", whereas "flounder" is closer in meaning to
"wallow". Second, "flounder" is hardly an upstart neologism -- the OED
has citations with close enough spelling and pronunciation to be
likely the same word as far back as 1625 -- if a word's been in usage
for clsoe on 400 years, only the most proscriptivist linguistic
reactionary could argue it's not valid. Lastly, "flounder" and
"founder" likely have different etymologies: this is unclear, since
the etymology of the verb "flounder" is somewhat obscure, but probably
by way of the verb "blunder" and regionalism "fludder", neither of
which have any apparent connection to either flat-fish or the verb
"founder".

How's about we talk about Nethack instead?

